1
00:00:02,288 --> 00:00:04,209
If you open the news today

2
00:00:04,269 --> 00:00:08,953
it feels like a brand-new artificial intelligence is born every twelve hours.

3
00:00:09,013 --> 00:00:11,653
ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude, DeepSeek, Llama, Grok...

4
00:00:11,713 --> 00:00:16,069
Trillion-dollar tech giants build datacenter cities powered by nuclear reactors

5
00:00:16,129 --> 00:00:19,728
open-source labs drop sudden shockwaves from across the globe

6
00:00:19,788 --> 00:00:25,018
and every single company claims their silicon mind is the smartest thing on Earth.

7
00:00:25,078 --> 00:00:30,050
But if you peel away the billion-dollar marketing and the endless brand names...

8
00:00:30,110 --> 00:00:32,126
what actually is an AI model?

9
00:00:32,186 --> 00:00:34,287
Are they completely different machines

10
00:00:34,347 --> 00:00:37,923
or are they all secretly running the exact same trick?

11
00:00:37,983 --> 00:00:46,084
Here is the biggest secret in computer science: whether an AI was trained in California by OpenAI or in Hangzhou by DeepSeek

12
00:00:46,144 --> 00:00:51,498
every frontier language model on this planet runs on the exact same three-step recipe.

13
00:00:51,558 --> 00:00:54,112
Step one: chop human words into numbers.

14
00:00:54,172 --> 00:00:59,738
Step two: multiply those numbers through billions of microscopic mathematical dials called weights.

15
00:00:59,798 --> 00:01:03,943
And step three: calculate the probability of what word comes next.

16
00:01:04,003 --> 00:01:04,645
That's it!

17
00:01:04,705 --> 00:01:06,658
But over the last two years

18
00:01:06,718 --> 00:01:10,380
this simple recipe split into four completely distinct species.

19
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:10,940
Welcome...

20
00:01:10,947 --> 00:01:12,518
to the Modern AI Zoo.

21
00:01:12,578 --> 00:01:18,151
Species Number One: The Fast Auctioneer — next-token predictors that talk at lightning speed.

22
00:01:18,211 --> 00:01:26,757
Species Number Two: The Chess Detective — reasoning models that pause to think in a secret scratchpad before they ever utter a word.

23
00:01:26,817 --> 00:01:36,112
Species Number Three: The Specialist Kitchen — Mixture-of-Experts models that save ninety-five percent of electricity by only waking up the chefs they need.

24
00:01:36,172 --> 00:01:40,988
And Species Number Four: The Autonomous Agent — models that don't just chat

25
00:01:41,048 --> 00:01:43,157
but have eyes, hands, and tools.

26
00:01:43,217 --> 00:01:44,975
To understand how they work

27
00:01:45,035 --> 00:01:52,051
we have to start at the absolute foundation: how does a cold piece of melted sand understand human language?

28
00:01:56,859 --> 00:01:58,437
Computers cannot read letters.

29
00:01:58,497 --> 00:02:05,438
A computer chip is literally just billions of microscopic switches that are either ON or OFF — ones and zeros.

30
00:02:05,498 --> 00:02:09,706
It has no idea what the letter A or the word strawberry means.

31
00:02:09,766 --> 00:02:10,287
In fact

32
00:02:10,347 --> 00:02:13,393
this explains the most famous AI bug in history!

33
00:02:13,453 --> 00:02:17,414
When you ask an older chatbot how many r's are in strawberry

34
00:02:17,474 --> 00:02:18,755
it proudly says Two!

35
00:02:18,815 --> 00:02:19,315
Why?

36
00:02:19,120 --> 00:02:21,980
Because the AI never saw the individual letters!

37
00:02:22,040 --> 00:02:28,758
A mechanical tool called a Tokenizer chopped the word into puzzle blocks using a process called Byte-Pair Encoding.

38
00:02:28,818 --> 00:02:32,035
The word strawberry becomes three separate tokens: S-T-R-A-W

39
00:02:32,095 --> 00:02:33,524
then A-W, then B-E-R-R-Y.

40
00:02:33,584 --> 00:02:35,811
The model knows berry has two r's

41
00:02:35,871 --> 00:02:38,187
but straw was just an opaque number!

42
00:02:38,247 --> 00:02:40,108
If it can't see the letters

43
00:02:40,168 --> 00:02:42,573
how does it understand what words mean?

44
00:02:42,633 --> 00:02:44,458
Enter the miracle of Embeddings!

45
00:02:44,518 --> 00:02:47,616
Imagine a colossal supermarket with thousands of aisles.

46
00:02:47,676 --> 00:02:50,528
The store manager doesn't shelve items alphabetically.

47
00:02:50,588 --> 00:02:55,793
Bananas don't sit next to Band-Aids just because they both start with the letter B!

48
00:02:55,853 --> 00:02:57,953
Bananas sit next to apples, oranges

49
00:02:58,013 --> 00:02:59,994
and mangoes in the fruit aisle.

50
00:03:00,054 --> 00:03:03,562
And dog food sits next to leashes in the pet aisle.

51
00:03:03,622 --> 00:03:06,876
In machine learning, this is called an Embedding Space.

52
00:03:06,936 --> 00:03:08,217
In models like GPT-4

53
00:03:08,277 --> 00:03:13,266
every single token is given a GPS coordinate made of over twelve thousand numbers!

54
00:03:13,326 --> 00:03:16,908
Each number represents a different dimension of meaning: sweetness

55
00:03:16,968 --> 00:03:19,426
furriness, historical era, or emotional warmth.

56
00:03:19,486 --> 00:03:21,824
Because words are now geometric coordinates

57
00:03:21,884 --> 00:03:24,773
you can literally do algebra with human thoughts!

58
00:03:24,833 --> 00:03:28,065
If you take the GPS coordinates of the word KING

59
00:03:28,125 --> 00:03:29,800
subtract the vector for MAN

60
00:03:29,860 --> 00:03:31,841
and add the vector for WOMAN...

61
00:03:31,901 --> 00:03:37,829
the coordinates glide straight across the supermarket space and land directly on the shelf for QUEEN!

62
00:03:37,889 --> 00:03:41,881
The computer doesn't know what it feels like to wear a crown.

63
00:03:41,941 --> 00:03:44,004
It just knows multidimensional geometry!

64
00:03:44,064 --> 00:03:45,523
And in modern Transformers

65
00:03:45,583 --> 00:03:49,664
these coordinates are alive: if the word bank sits next to river

66
00:03:49,724 --> 00:03:51,489
its vector steers toward water

67
00:03:51,549 --> 00:03:54,840
if it sits next to robbery, it steers toward cash.

68
00:03:59,785 --> 00:04:02,674
Before 2017, AI had a terrible memory problem.

69
00:04:02,734 --> 00:04:05,439
Models read sentences one word at a time

70
00:04:05,499 --> 00:04:08,795
like a tired reader looking through a drinking straw.

71
00:04:08,855 --> 00:04:12,505
By the time they reached the end of a long paragraph

72
00:04:12,565 --> 00:04:15,700
they completely forgot what happened at the beginning!

73
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:16,259
Then

74
00:04:16,074 --> 00:04:22,066
eight researchers at Google published a paper with an audacious title: Attention Is All You Need.

75
00:04:22,126 --> 00:04:23,787
Instead of reading sequentially

76
00:04:23,847 --> 00:04:29,308
what if the model could look at every single word in an entire document simultaneously

77
00:04:29,368 --> 00:04:30,061
in parallel?

78
00:04:30,121 --> 00:04:32,665
This was the invention of the Transformer

79
00:04:32,725 --> 00:04:34,231
and it changed everything.

80
00:04:34,291 --> 00:04:37,680
How does a Transformer know which words belong together?

81
00:04:37,740 --> 00:04:40,905
Through an elegant mathematical dance called Query, Key

82
00:04:40,965 --> 00:04:41,596
and Value.

83
00:04:41,656 --> 00:04:45,867
Imagine every word in a sentence is at a crowded cocktail party.

84
00:04:45,927 --> 00:04:51,557
The word it holds up a megaphone called a Query: Who does the word it refer to?

85
00:04:51,617 --> 00:04:54,983
Every other word wears a name badge called a Key.

86
00:04:55,043 --> 00:04:57,970
The word socket says: I am a wall outlet.

87
00:04:58,030 --> 00:05:02,179
The word robot says: I am a mechanical machine that was tired.

88
00:05:02,239 --> 00:05:05,689
The math multiplies the Query by every Key simultaneously.

89
00:05:05,749 --> 00:05:08,415
Socket gets a twelve percent relevance score.

90
00:05:08,475 --> 00:05:11,203
But robot scores an eighty-eight percent match!

91
00:05:11,263 --> 00:05:12,962
Because of that high score

92
00:05:13,022 --> 00:05:18,583
robot opens its briefcase of information — the Value — and hands it straight to it.

93
00:05:18,643 --> 00:05:21,440
Now, the model knows exactly what it means!

94
00:05:21,500 --> 00:05:24,105
Modern models don't just use one spotlight.

95
00:05:24,165 --> 00:05:26,885
GPT-4 runs ninety-six attention heads simultaneously!

96
00:05:26,945 --> 00:05:28,544
One flashlight tracks grammar

97
00:05:28,604 --> 00:05:30,203
another tracks emotional tone

98
00:05:30,263 --> 00:05:31,547
another connects pronouns

99
00:05:31,607 --> 00:05:33,582
and another tracks technical facts.

100
00:05:33,642 --> 00:05:37,684
Once all ninety-six flashlights finish analyzing every word in parallel

101
00:05:37,744 --> 00:05:44,542
the Transformer does something deceptively simple: it calculates a probability for what single word should come next.

102
00:05:44,602 --> 00:05:46,761
Eighty-nine percent chance it's recharged

103
00:05:46,821 --> 00:05:48,013
seven percent exploded

104
00:05:48,073 --> 00:05:49,818
zero-point-zero-zero-one percent danced.

105
00:05:49,878 --> 00:05:52,959
It picks the best option and repeats the loop.

106
00:05:53,019 --> 00:05:56,154
This is Species One: the fast, conversational chatbot.

107
00:06:00,875 --> 00:06:01,456
For years

108
00:06:01,516 --> 00:06:05,353
language models had a fatal flaw: they were addicted to speed!

109
00:06:05,413 --> 00:06:09,028
A standard chatbot operates like an impulsive game-show contestant.

110
00:06:09,088 --> 00:06:12,344
Because it generates words in a continuous forward rush

111
00:06:12,404 --> 00:06:14,505
it answers hard math, chess tactics

112
00:06:14,565 --> 00:06:18,888
and legal logic in twenty milliseconds — and gets them completely wrong!

113
00:06:18,948 --> 00:06:23,225
Ask it: A bat and a ball cost a dollar and ten cents total

114
00:06:23,285 --> 00:06:26,154
the bat costs a dollar more than the ball.

115
00:06:26,214 --> 00:06:27,712
How much is the ball?

116
00:06:27,772 --> 00:06:29,567
It instantly blurts: Ten cents!

117
00:06:29,627 --> 00:06:30,127
Wrong!

118
00:06:29,993 --> 00:06:30,968
It's five cents.

119
00:06:31,028 --> 00:06:35,783
The model failed because it had no scratchpad to pause and do the algebra.

120
00:06:35,843 --> 00:06:38,183
Enter Species Number Two: Reasoning Models!

121
00:06:38,243 --> 00:06:41,097
Revolutionized by OpenAI's o1 and China's DeepSeek-R1.

122
00:06:41,157 --> 00:06:44,145
When you give a reasoning model a hard problem

123
00:06:44,205 --> 00:06:48,199
it doesn't blurt out the first token that pops into its head.

124
00:06:48,259 --> 00:06:49,816
It takes a deep breath.

125
00:06:49,876 --> 00:06:50,882
Behind the scenes

126
00:06:50,942 --> 00:06:54,378
it opens a secret internal scratchpad called thinking tokens.

127
00:06:54,438 --> 00:06:56,904
It writes out equations, tests edge cases

128
00:06:56,964 --> 00:06:58,819
breaks the problem into sub-goals

129
00:06:58,879 --> 00:07:04,521
and verifies its own math before it ever shows you a single word of its final answer!

130
00:07:04,581 --> 00:07:06,771
It traded instant speed for deliberate

131
00:07:06,831 --> 00:07:08,255
human-like systematic thinking.

132
00:07:08,315 --> 00:07:11,079
And here is the true breakthrough: self-correction!

133
00:07:11,139 --> 00:07:16,408
Watch what happens when the reasoning model hits a dead end in its secret scratchpad.

134
00:07:16,468 --> 00:07:18,293
It doesn't crash or hallucinate!

135
00:07:18,353 --> 00:07:21,155
It literally reads its own thought, says: Wait

136
00:07:21,215 --> 00:07:23,592
that logic will fail, let me backtrack

137
00:07:23,652 --> 00:07:26,454
erases the branch, and tries a new hypothesis!

138
00:07:26,514 --> 00:07:29,771
Even crazier: nobody taught DeepSeek-R1 how to do this!

139
00:07:29,831 --> 00:07:33,020
Using a technique called Group Relative Policy Optimization

140
00:07:33,080 --> 00:07:37,551
researchers simply rewarded the model when its final math answer was correct.

141
00:07:37,611 --> 00:07:40,801
By running millions of trial-and-error games against itself

142
00:07:40,861 --> 00:07:44,184
the AI discovered on its own that talking to itself

143
00:07:44,244 --> 00:07:44,744
pausing

144
00:07:44,639 --> 00:07:48,104
and erasing its mistakes was the secret to super-intelligence!

145
00:07:52,552 --> 00:07:54,598
As artificial intelligence grew smarter

146
00:07:54,658 --> 00:07:57,602
it ran straight into a wall of pure physics!

147
00:07:57,662 --> 00:07:59,378
In a traditional dense model

148
00:07:59,438 --> 00:08:04,697
every single parameter inside the brain must activate for every single syllable it speaks.

149
00:08:04,757 --> 00:08:07,544
If a model has six hundred billion parameters

150
00:08:07,604 --> 00:08:10,736
and you ask it what is the capital of France

151
00:08:10,796 --> 00:08:17,284
you have to blast electricity through all six hundred billion connections just to spit out the word Paris!

152
00:08:17,344 --> 00:08:20,611
Datacenters began consuming the electricity of entire cities.

153
00:08:20,671 --> 00:08:22,686
It was unsustainable, wildly expensive

154
00:08:22,746 --> 00:08:25,901
and threatened to freeze AI progress in its tracks.

155
00:08:25,961 --> 00:08:29,902
So engineers turned to an architectural masterpiece: Mixture of Experts

156
00:08:29,962 --> 00:08:30,486
or MoE.

157
00:08:30,546 --> 00:08:33,880
Think of a bustling kitchen with sixty-four master chefs.

158
00:08:33,940 --> 00:08:36,517
When a customer orders a bowl of pasta

159
00:08:36,577 --> 00:08:39,859
you don't wake up the sushi chef, the pastry chef

160
00:08:39,919 --> 00:08:42,579
the barbecue pitmaster, and the French saucier!

161
00:08:42,639 --> 00:08:43,987
That would be madness!

162
00:08:44,047 --> 00:08:46,992
You only tap the pasta chef on the shoulder!

163
00:08:47,052 --> 00:08:48,190
In an MoE model

164
00:08:48,250 --> 00:08:55,465
a lightning-fast router evaluates each incoming token and activates only the top expert subnetworks best suited for the job.

165
00:08:55,525 --> 00:08:57,945
The rest of the brain sleeps peacefully

166
00:08:58,005 --> 00:08:59,901
saving enormous amounts of energy.

167
00:08:59,961 --> 00:09:03,407
This architectural trick sparked a global technological earthquake.

168
00:09:03,467 --> 00:09:04,202
In DeepSeek-V3

169
00:09:04,262 --> 00:09:10,278
there are six hundred and seventy-one billion total parameters — giving it vast encyclopedia-level knowledge.

170
00:09:10,338 --> 00:09:12,668
But for any single word it generates

171
00:09:12,728 --> 00:09:15,170
it only fires thirty-seven billion parameters!

172
00:09:15,230 --> 00:09:18,594
That is a ninety-five percent discount on computing power!

173
00:09:18,654 --> 00:09:23,539
While Silicon Valley spent hundreds of millions of dollars brute-forcing monolithic models

174
00:09:23,599 --> 00:09:31,698
open-weights researchers in China proved that elegant mathematical routing could match the world's best benchmarks at a fraction of the cost.

175
00:09:31,758 --> 00:09:34,546
The future of AI isn't just bigger computers.

176
00:09:34,606 --> 00:09:35,827
It's smarter algorithms!

177
00:09:40,459 --> 00:09:45,645
And that brings us to the fourth and most powerful species of all: Autonomous Agents.

178
00:09:45,705 --> 00:09:46,548
Up until now

179
00:09:46,608 --> 00:09:49,666
artificial intelligence was just a brain in a jar.

180
00:09:49,726 --> 00:09:51,259
You asked it a question

181
00:09:51,319 --> 00:09:53,299
and it politely handed you text.

182
00:09:53,359 --> 00:09:57,775
But what good is a recipe if the chef doesn't have hands to cook?

183
00:09:57,835 --> 00:10:02,515
What happens when you give that brain eyes to look at your computer screen

184
00:10:02,575 --> 00:10:04,342
hands to type terminal commands

185
00:10:04,402 --> 00:10:06,382
and permission to click a mouse?

186
00:10:06,442 --> 00:10:09,229
That is the revolution of modern coding agents.

187
00:10:09,289 --> 00:10:11,863
The AI doesn't just write suggestions anymore.

188
00:10:11,923 --> 00:10:15,466
It sits in your chair, runs the code, reads the errors

189
00:10:15,526 --> 00:10:17,029
and gets the job done!

190
00:10:17,089 --> 00:10:18,622
How does an agent work?

191
00:10:18,682 --> 00:10:22,423
Through an infinite feedback loop called ReAct: Reason plus Action.

192
00:10:22,483 --> 00:10:23,501
Step one: OBSERVE.

193
00:10:23,561 --> 00:10:26,796
The agent inspects your computer screen or terminal log.

194
00:10:26,856 --> 00:10:27,845
Step two: REASON.

195
00:10:27,905 --> 00:10:30,663
Using thinking tokens, it plans its next move.

196
00:10:30,723 --> 00:10:31,683
Step three: ACT.

197
00:10:31,743 --> 00:10:34,684
It calls a tool — executing a terminal command

198
00:10:34,744 --> 00:10:37,054
searching the web, or editing a file.

199
00:10:37,114 --> 00:10:38,434
And step four: VERIFY.

200
00:10:38,494 --> 00:10:40,474
It checks if the command worked.

201
00:10:40,534 --> 00:10:42,484
If the compiler throws an error

202
00:10:42,544 --> 00:10:46,058
the agent doesn't panic and give up, it reads the bug

203
00:10:46,118 --> 00:10:47,166
diagnoses the fault

204
00:10:47,226 --> 00:10:49,954
and loops right back to try another solution.

205
00:10:50,014 --> 00:10:56,419
AI has transformed from an oracle that answers questions into an autonomous partner that actually solves problems.

206
00:10:56,479 --> 00:10:57,439
Eighty years ago

207
00:10:57,499 --> 00:11:03,383
Alan Turing looked at a room full of vacuum tubes and asked if a machine could ever think.

208
00:11:03,443 --> 00:11:03,943
Today

209
00:11:03,773 --> 00:11:08,761
whether through massive American superclusters or elegant open-weight algorithms across the globe

210
00:11:08,821 --> 00:11:10,500
we have unlocked the secret.

211
00:11:10,560 --> 00:11:19,709
Think about the poetry of what humanity has achieved: we took the most common mineral on our planet — ordinary beach sand — melted it into quartz

212
00:11:19,769 --> 00:11:22,997
etched billions of microscopic switches with ultraviolet light

213
00:11:23,057 --> 00:11:25,543
and taught electricity how to pay attention

214
00:11:25,603 --> 00:11:27,311
how to reason through puzzles

215
00:11:27,371 --> 00:11:29,293
and how to speak our language.

216
00:11:29,353 --> 00:11:30,855
AI is not black magic.

217
00:11:30,915 --> 00:11:32,741
It is humanity's greatest mirror.

218
00:11:32,801 --> 00:11:35,199
And our journey into the silicon mind...

219
00:11:35,259 --> 00:11:36,520
has only just begun.
